<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Weekend.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Productive: Use basic words or phrases to identify time words.<br>Interactive: Exchange simple questions and answers about the current day.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Can identify specific time-related words, such as days of the week and the weekend, in simple sentences (e.g., "Saturday and Sunday are the weekends." "Today is Tuesday.").<br>Can exchange simple questions and answers about the current day, using basic time-related words (e.g., "What day is it today?" "Today is Monday.").<br>Can respond to questions about time using basic words and phrases to indicate days (e.g., "What is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Wednesday." or "It's the weekend.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads students in a "Days of the Week" song with corresponding actions (e.g., marching for Monday, dancing for Saturday). Each day is reinforced with a large, colorful flashcard. Students repeat the names of the days chorally, pointing to the flashcards as they are called out.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up the flashcard for the current day and says, "Today is [Monday/Tuesday/etc.]." The teacher then holds up "Saturday" and "Sunday" flashcards and says, "Saturday and Sunday are the weekend. The weekend is relaxing!" The teacher models the pronunciation of /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day," exaggerating the sounds. Students repeat the phrases "Today is..." and "It's the weekend" after the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows "Wednesday" card: "Is Wednesday the weekend?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows "Saturday" card: "Is Saturday the weekend?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a calendar: "What day is today?" (Expected answer: "Today is [actual day].")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, animated video or a teacher-led interactive display showing different children doing activities on each day of the week, clearly labeling "Monday," "Tuesday," etc., and distinguishing "the weekend." The video highlights simple sentences like "Today is Tuesday. It is busy." or "Saturday and Sunday are the weekend. It is exciting!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Exchanging questions and answers about the current day using simple phrases like "Today is Monday." Using time-related vocabulary by identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the video/shows the display. After each day, the teacher pauses and asks: "Is this Monday?" (Yes/No). Students respond chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Show me the weekend!" Students point to Saturday and Sunday on the screen or flashcards.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What day is before Monday?" (Sunday!) or "What day is after Tuesday?" (Wednesday!). Students respond by calling out the day.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using "Noun + verb + noun" (e.g., "Today is Monday."), "Present simple" (e.g., "It is the weekend."), and "WH-questions" (e.g., "What day is today?"). Key vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day, today, tomorrow, be (am, is, are). Focus on /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Sentence Completion"</b><br>The teacher shows a large flashcard of a day (e.g., Monday). The teacher says slowly, "Today is..." and gestures for students to complete the sentence chorally. Students respond: "MONDAY!" The teacher repeats with different days. Then, the teacher holds up "Saturday" and says, "It is the..." Students respond: "WEEKEND!" This activity reinforces the Noun + verb + noun structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Question and Answer Drill"</b><br>The teacher holds up a flashcard for a day (e.g., Wednesday) and asks the class, "What day is it today?" Students respond chorally: "Today is Wednesday." Then the teacher asks, "What day is tomorrow?" Students respond: "Tomorrow is Thursday." The teacher emphasizes correct pronunciation. This practices WH-questions and the use of 'today' and 'tomorrow'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Day Card Exchange"</b><br>Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 7 small flashcards with the days of the week. Student A picks a card (without showing it) and asks Student B, "What day is it today?" Student B guesses a day, for example, "Is it Monday?" Student A responds "Yes, it is Monday!" or "No, it is not Monday." Then they swap roles. The focus is on the simple exchange and using "Today is..." and "It is not..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Partner Conversation – "My Day, Your Day." Students will engage in a simple, guided conversation, asking and answering about days of the week and identifying the weekend, incorporating basic adjectives and politeness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students imagine they are asking a friend about their week, focusing on "today" and "tomorrow" within the context of "All Week Long!" and identifying "the weekend."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up. Each student receives a "My Day" card with one specific day of the week and a simple picture clue (e.g., a school building for Monday-Friday, kids playing for Saturday/Sunday).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A starts by asking Student B: "Good morning! What day is it today?" (Sociolinguistic: using polite expressions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B looks at their card and responds: "Good morning! Today is [day on card]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A then asks: "Is it the weekend?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B responds: "Yes, it is the weekend. It is relaxing!" or "No, it is not the weekend. It is busy!" (Incorporating adjectives like 'relaxing' or 'busy' and pronunciation of /w/ and /d/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, Student A asks: "What day is tomorrow?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B answers: "Tomorrow is [next day]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles and repeat the conversation. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging clear speaking.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Day, Your Day" partner conversation. The teacher notes down participation, intelligibility, and correct use of target language for a few pairs at a time.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "My Day, Your Day" partner conversation for the teacher or a small group. They will exchange simple questions and answers about the current day and identify the weekend using the learned phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use "today," "tomorrow," "weekend," and days of the week correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Accuracy:</b> Students use "Today is [day]" and "It is the weekend/It is not the weekend" correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students engage in the conversation politely (e.g., using "Good morning").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a simple paper slip to complete before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a happy face 😊 or a sad face 😔: "I can say the days of the week."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circle YES or NO: "I can ask 'What day is it today?'"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (facilitated by teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you today when we talked about the days?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little difficult for you today?" (e.g., remembering "tomorrow" or "weekend").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students meet the specific speaking objective? Were they able to identify time words and exchange simple questions and answers about the current day?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging 1st graders in speaking activities? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, paired work).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to ensure smooth progression from speaking to writing these concepts? (e.g., provide sentence frames for writing, focus on spelling the days of the week).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today, students practiced speaking about the days of the week and the weekend. In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), they will use these same words and phrases to write simple sentences about the days, like "Today is Monday" or "Saturday is the weekend."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>